# Geometry Meets Arithmetic: An Interactive Tour

A right triangle asks for $a^2+b^2=c^2$; a factor certificate asks for $ab=N$. The energy $E=(a^2+b^2-c^2)^2+(ab-N)^2$ makes both demands at once. Begin with the [background on Pythagorean triples](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pythagorean_triple), then manipulate the exact spectrum below.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

## Check a certificate yourself

Every term is a square, so zero energy forces both equations separately. Try $(3,4,5;12)$, $(5,12,13;60)$, and a deliberately incorrect triple.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{demo:1}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal the zero-energy proof</summary>

If $x^2+y^2=0$ for integers $x,y$, then both nonnegative squares vanish. Taking $x=a^2+b^2-c^2$ and $y=ab-N$ proves that $E=0$ exactly when the triple is Pythagorean and its legs multiply to $N$. If also $1<a<N$, the identity $N=ab$ certifies the proper divisor $a$.

</details>

## Curvature without calculus

The target spectrum has an exact symmetric second difference. Change the step size and see why every nonzero displacement gives strict convexity.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{visualization:0}}

{{algorithm:0}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal the second-difference derivation</summary>

Write $d=ab-N$ and $C=(a^2+b^2-c^2)^2$. Then the energies at $N+h$, $N-h$, and $N$ are $C+(d-h)^2$, $C+(d+h)^2$, and $C+d^2$. Subtraction leaves $(d-h)^2+(d+h)^2-2d^2=2h^2$.

</details>

## From isolated triples to a tree

Primitive positive triples form the [Berggren tree](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tree_of_primitive_Pythagorean_triples). Its first generation already contains a zero-energy certificate for $60$.

{{visualization:2}}

{{algorithm:2}}

{{demo:0}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal the important limitation</summary>

Strict convexity varies the target while fixing the triple. A tree move changes the triple instead. Therefore the curvature theorem does not by itself prove convergence of greedy tree descent; local minima and path bounds remain separate research questions.

</details>

## Certificate gallery and direct minimization

For any fixed triple, its unique preferred target is simply $ab$. This remains true even for a non-Pythagorean triple; the geometric residual then raises the minimum above zero.

